// MCU_castalia_penta LVS control file.
//
// ============================================================================
// NET-TEXT-ONLY LAYER FOR THE VDD_SW VIRTUAL-CONNECT LABELS (2026-08-26).
// ============================================================================
// This block is the PENTA-side half of the fix that pvs/lvs_tile_ctl carries
// for the tile.  It is not optional and it is not cosmetic.
//
// tcl/MCU_castalia_penta_lvs_netlist.tcl builds the chip label file by reading
// pvs/hart_tile.lvslabels and re-emitting each piece four times, once per
// hardened corner tile.  It copies the tile line's LAYER FIELD VERBATIM
// (lindex $p 0 at the read, %s at the write).  On 2026-08-26 05:32 the tile
// dump moved its 407 "VDD_SW:" pieces from GDS layer 131 to layer 231, so the
// chip dump now emits 1628 texts on layer 231 as well.
//
// Layer 231 has NO LAYER_MAP in the TSMC deck (verified: no 231 anywhere in
// kit/PVS/LVS/pvs.lvs).  Without the three statements below those 1628 texts
// are read and thrown away: they attach to nothing, the 1628 VDD_SW metal-1
// pieces are never unified, and VDD_SW matching collapses into hundreds of
// unmatched nets.
//
// THE FAILURE IS SILENT.  lvs.sh's sentinel-arming grep looks for the two
// "VDD:" / "VSS:" short sentinels, and those are hard-coded to layer 131 at
// MCU_castalia_penta_lvs_netlist.tcl:299-300.  They stay on 131, the grep
// still prints ARMED, and the run reports a plausible-looking MISMATCH with
// no indication that the text layer went dark.  Do not remove these lines.
//
// WHY 231 AT ALL (the tile's reason, inherited here): the deck says of layer
// 131 BOTH
//     TEXT_LAYER      metal1_text;   // name the net
//     PORT -TEXT_LAYER metal1_text;  // AND declare a port
// (pvs.lvs:1246-1249), so every VDD_SW text on 131 also declared a top-cell
// PORT.  On the penta that produced four phantom layout pins VDD_SW_H1..H4,
// reported as "** missing pin **" against the schematic.  Pegasus keeps the
// two roles on separate rules, so a text_layer'd + attached layer that is NOT
// a port -text_layer names the net without declaring a port.  The four
// lvs_cpoint bindings below still resolve: they match by NET name, and the
// net is still named VDD_SW_H<n> by the text.
layer_def vddsw_net_text 231;
text_layer vddsw_net_text;
attach vddsw_net_text metal1;

// ============================================================================
// BUS-BIT DELIMITER NORMALISATION (2026-08-26).
// ============================================================================
// Copied from pvs/lvs_tile_ctl, where the syntax was established the hard way
// (see that file's note: the option letters go INSIDE the quoted string, the
// rule parser eats one backslash level so a literal bracket is written as the
// character class [[] / []] and the backreference is doubled, and it must be
// ONE statement because a text edited by one layout_rename_text is not
// re-examined by the next).
//
// LVS_COMPARE_PORT_NAMES is YES in the deck and compares the layout pin TEXT
// against the schematic pin NAME character for character.  strmout writes the
// OA/LEF names prt1[0]..prt6[7]; lvs.sh already rewrites the CDL side to <n>.
// Measured on the cpr8 compare: 48 entries in UNMATCHED LAYOUT PIN LABELS and
// 48 in UNMATCHED SCHEMATIC PIN LABELS, every one of them a prtN[b] vs prtN<b>
// notation difference on a pin that had already matched by net.
//
// Notation only: base name and bit index are both preserved, so a genuine
// swap (prt1<3> labelled onto prt1<4>) is still reported.  The tile's
// positive control (pvs/hart_tile_poscontrol/run_namecontrol.sh) proves the
// name check stays live under this rewrite.
layout_rename_text "/[[]([0-9]+)[]]/<\\1>/g";

// ============================================================================
// REPORT SIZE (2026-08-26).
// ============================================================================
// KEPT AT -all, deliberately.  The 2.5 GB lvs.rep.cls this block used to
// produce was NOT caused by this rule: it was caused by 251,484 unmatched
// schematic nets, 99.25% of them literally named VNW or VPW, from a CDL built
// eleven hours before lvs.sh learned to bind the well ports on all A10TH
// cells.  With that fixed the same -all report is 46 MB.
//
// A numeric limit is the wrong lever here anyway.  Per pegasusref
// lvs_report_max: "A list in the LVS report is ELIMINATED if it contains more
// items than the specified limit" -- a whole list disappears with no marker,
// which is precisely the shape of failure this project keeps paying for.
//
// The -mismatched_net_limit is written out explicitly at its DEFAULT value so
// the cap is visible rather than implicit: the per-net terminal lists in
// MISMATCHED NETS are truncated at 100 entries whether or not this rule names
// the number.  Raise it only for a specific investigation.
lvs_report_max -all -mismatched_net_limit 100;

// ============================================================================
// COMPARE SETUP
// ============================================================================
// hart_tile is NOT an hcell here: the layout is a flat chip run and the source
// netlist has the FULL tile netlist appended (see
// gen_MCU_castalia_penta_lvs_collateral.sh, "WHY THE CONCATENATION").
lvs_exclude_hcell hart_tile;

// CPR3 RENUMBER: the four HARDENED corner tiles are Xmcu0/Xhart1..Xhart4.
// Xmcu0/Xhart0 is the SOFT orchestrator -- no switched rail, no label, no
// cpoint.  Keep these four in sync with the VDD_SW_H<n> texts written by
// tcl/MCU_castalia_penta_lvs_netlist.tcl; a name/index skew here is a phantom
// MISMATCH, not an error.
lvs_cpoint VDD_SW_H1 "Xmcu0/Xhart1/VDD_SW";
lvs_cpoint VDD_SW_H2 "Xmcu0/Xhart2/VDD_SW";
lvs_cpoint VDD_SW_H3 "Xmcu0/Xhart3/VDD_SW";
lvs_cpoint VDD_SW_H4 "Xmcu0/Xhart4/VDD_SW";
